A||N||7||kornholio||trunk release relay is bad?||||5
Z||000000||kornholio||11-27-1999||07:17 PM||||Another problem...<BR>For the last couple days, when I push my trunk release button, there is a clicking sound coming from under the dash near the steering column, but the trunk doesn't "pop."  It clicks once when I push the button, and clicks again when I release the button.  This clicking sound is not coming from the switch itself.  Sometimes the trunk pops on the first push of the button and sometimes I could be pushing it 10 to 20 times with no pop.  I also sometimes get pissed off and give the switch a good whack which usually pops the trunk.<P>I was wondering if whether my switch is bad or my trunk release relay is bad.  The reason I didn't suspect the switch in the first place is because there is a clicking sound.<P>Also, this just happened after I installed a remote starter.  I looked in the Haynes manual and it shows that the yellow starter wire also runs to the trunk release relay.  I verified this wire by cutting it.  When it is cut, the trunk does not pop and the starter doesn't crank.<P><BR>Where exactly is the trunk release relay and what does it look like?  Is it a costly part?<P>Thanks.<p>[This message has been edited by kornholio (edited 11-27-1999).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||TOM||11-27-1999||08:42 PM||||I would say the switch is good if the relay clicks. And if the relay clicks it might also be good.<BR>It is located under the dash on the right side of the column. It is a small square relay plugged into a connector. I think it is white of clear. It has three wires to it. Try jumping the connectors with the relay out and see if the trunk solonoid click everytime.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-27-1999||11:18 PM||||goto the <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl</A>  page and go into the ogre cave. I cover testing this there.<P>quick test. switch the horn relay to the trunk relay socket. they are interchangeable. if it now works the trunk relay was bad.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||11-28-1999||07:20 AM||||You can always bypass the relay. It seems that this relay doesn't funtion as a  normal relay but as a safety device. In a standard shift if the engine is running you have to have the e-brake on to open the trunk. All of the power to operate the trunk release and the relay runs through the switch.The relay is sort of like an AND gate.||||reg||1
Z||000004||kornholio||11-28-1999||09:30 AM||||Is the horn relay located by the passenger's foot compartment?||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||11-28-1999||10:47 AM||||I can't believe Ogre didn't mention needing to check the ground contacts on the trunk release circuit. Maybe he does in "the cave" article. Also, check fuses. Sounds weird, but once with my 85 GT, the relay would audibly click and sometimes even pop the lid, but the fuse was burnt out.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ggarsk||11-28-1999||08:28 PM||||A quick note about the trunk release. If you have a wing you might not want to have it working. A thief can break out your window,open your trunk and unbolt the wing. Mines not hooked for that reason.||||reg||1
Z||000007||kornholio||11-28-1999||08:39 PM||||It probably is the ground contacts.  I have the trunk hooked up to my alarm so that I can pop the trunk with the remote.  I pushed the button, but the trunk did not pop.  I pushed down on the decklid, then pushed the button on the remote and the solenoid worked.  So how can I fix this problem?  I know that the trunk carpet is not blocking the contact.  Thanks for all the help guys.||||reg||14
